        <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">2</td>
        <td>
          <strong>The Neural-Symbolic Query Fabric: Weaving Machine Learning into the Core of Database Operations</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po2')">Toggle Abstract</a> Kathiresan Jayabalan (Mastercard)*; Anand Chinnakannan (Walmart)<br />
          <div id="po2" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
              Databases manage data with precision but lack learned intelligence, while ML models offer predictive power but operate as external black boxes.
The Neural-Symbolic Query Fabric integrates compact ML models as first-class citizens within database query engines, enabling native semantic search,
anomaly detection, and predictive filtering without costly data movement—transforming databases into intelligent, proactive data partners.
          </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

          <tr>
            <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">5</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Enabling Easy, Correct, and Reproducible Evaluation of Distributed Storage Systems With Evaluation as a Service</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po5')">Toggle Abstract</a> Shawn Zimmermann (University at Buffalo)*<br />
          <div id="po5" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
              We introduce Evaluation as a Service (EaaS), the first systematic, end-to-end framework that enables easy, correct, and reproducible evaluation of distributed storage systems during research development. Our design insight is to automate the evaluation pipeline, minimizing human intervention while maximizing the coverage of the testing space. Reduced human involvement enables a hands-off approach, making evaluation easy and smooth.

          </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

            <tr>
                <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">6</td>
        <td>
          <strong>BEACON: Budget-Aware Entity Matching Across Domains</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po6')">Toggle Abstract</a> Nicholas Pulsone (Worcester Polytechnic Institute)*; Roee Shraga (Worcester Polytechnic Institute); Greg Goren (eBay Research)<br />
          <div id="po6" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
              Entity Matching (EM)--the task of determining whether two data records refer to the same real-world entity--is a core task in data integration. Recent advances in deep learning have set a new standard for EM, particularly through fine-tuning Pretrained Language Models (PLMs) and, more recently, Large Language Models (LLMs). However, fine-tuning typically requires large amounts of labeled data, which are expensive and time-consuming to obtain. In the context of e-commerce matching, labeling scarcity varies widely across domains, raising the question of how to intelligently train accurate domain-specific EM models with limited labeled data. In this work we assume users have only limited amount of labels for a specific target domain but have access to labeled data from other domains. We introduce BEACON, a distribution-aware, budget-aware framework for low-resource EM across domains. BEACON leverages the insight that embedding representations of pairwise candidate matches can guide the effective selection of out-of-domain samples under limited in-domain supervision. We conduct extensive experiments across multiple domain-partitioned datasets derived from established EM benchmarks, demonstrating that BEACON consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods under different training budgets.
          </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

