                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">13</td>
        <td>
          <strong>SAVeD: Semantically Aware Version Discovery</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po13')">Toggle Abstract</a> Artem Frenk (Worcester Polytechnic Institute)*; 
Roee Shraga (Worcester Polytechnic Institute)<br />
          <div id="po13" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
              Our work introduces SAVeD (Semantically Aware Version Detection), a contrastive learning-based framework for identifying versions of structured datasets without relying on metadata, labels, or integration-based assumptions. SAVeD addresses a common challenge in data science of repeated labor due to a difficulty of similar work or transformations on datasets. SAVeD employs a modified SimCLR pipeline, generating augmented table views through random transformations (e.g., row deletion, encoding perturbations). These views are embedded via a custom transformer encoder and contrasted in latent space to optimize semantic similarity. Our model learns to minimize distances between augmented views of the same dataset and maximize those between unrelated tables. We evaluate performance using validation accuracy and separation, defined respectively as the proportion of correctly classified version/non-version pairs on a hold-out set, and the difference between average similarities of versioned and non-versioned tables (defined by a benchmark, and not provided to the model).<br/>Our experiments span five canonical datasets from the Semantic Versioning in Databases Benchmark, and demonstrate substantial gains post-training. SAVeD achieves significantly higher accuracy on completely unseen tables in, and a significant boost in separation scores, confirming its capability to distinguish semantically altered versions. Compared to untrained baselines, Small Language Models, and prior state-of-theart dataset-discovery methods like Starmie, our custom encoder achieves competitive or superior results.
             </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                     <tr>
                        <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">14</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Adaptive Timeout Selection for Consensus Protocols</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po14')">Toggle Abstract</a> Kevin Dharmawan (Stony Brook University)*; Prajwal Annigeri (Stony Brook University); Mohammad Amiri (Stony Brook University)<br />
          <div id="po14" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
              Timeout selection is a critical parameter in the performance and robustness of consensus protocols. Static or heuristically chosen timeout values often fail to adapt to dynamic workloads, heterogeneous server capabilities, and varying network conditions, leading to increased latency, reduced throughput, or unnecessary leader changes. Our work explores a learning-based framework for dynamically selecting timeout values that can be applied on top of any timer-based consensus protocol.<br/><br/>Our approach relies on server-side metrics that are readily observable during protocol execution, including leader identity, current timeout values, achieved throughput, observed latency, and incoming request demand. These metrics are used as inputs to a learning agent that periodically selects timeout values. The same signals are also used to define reward functions that capture performance objectives such as throughput, latency, and stability. The selected timeouts may be applied globally through consensus or locally by individual replicas.<br/><br/>Training a learning agent in a distributed system raises challenges due to partial observability, heterogeneous conditions, and the presence of noisy or adversarial data. We investigate a weighted aggregation framework for combining training data across replicas. At one extreme, nodes rely solely on local observations; at the other, all replicas’ data are treated equally. Intermediate weighting schemes allow nodes to prioritize local measurements while still leveraging shared information. To improve robustness, we consider aggregation functions that tolerate faulty or malicious inputs.<br/><br/>To capture a wide range of performance and failure scenarios, we model network delay, network failures, server failures, and heterogeneous server capabilities using a unified peer-to-peer delay abstraction. This abstraction represents the end-to-end interaction between replicas and captures the combined effects of communication latency, processing variability, and transient faults, allowing diverse delay and failure sources to be represented within a single model.
             </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

