            <p>
            Learned indexes are strong competitors to classical indexes like B+Trees due to efficient query performance and low space utilization. They operate by replacing the internal nodes of the index with a hierarchy of machine learning models that capture the data distribution. However, to achieve high space savings in practical indexing structures, learned indexes require a sorted version of the underlying data. We therefore explore lightweight mapping structures that can accurately translate the sorted positions predicted by the learned index to their actual physical locations in the base data. We focus on structures that implement this sorted-to-physical mapping by storing the permutation and balancing the space–time tradeoffs involved.<br/>Our initial work explored wavelet tree-based representations of this permutation to reduce memory footprint, but the traditional structure incurred high cache misses due to binary splits at each level. We extended this to a multiway variant, but this added complexity and required higher memory due to metadata needed for efficient access. Because of these tradeoffs, this structure could not surpass state-of-the-art permutation storage.<br/><br/>To address these challenges, we introduce a new data structure, Constellation Maps, which models the permutation in 2-D space. A set of points is represented as (i, p[i]) implying that the i-th sorted element exists at position p[i] in the original data. We encode these points using a set of lines (slope and intercept) along with a small error (a few bits). When the data exhibits structure, very few lines can map all points (e.g., a sorted group requires a single line through the origin). Moreover, if most points map to a few lines, these hot lines can be efficiently cached, leading to extremely fast access.<br/><br/>However, finding the best set of lines is computationally difficult. Linear solvers exist but do not scale to large datasets, and different line-drawing algorithms model specific distributions best. We currently explore multiple methods: RANSAC (a consensus-based greedy algorithm), recursively fitted linear regression, a greedy line-sampling approach, and a grid-voting algorithm using the Hough transform. Across these methods, selecting sampling strategies, budgets, stopping criteria, and loss functions is nontrivial. While the problem resembles set cover, the interaction between error bounds, coverage skew, and access efficiency makes it substantially harder and requires adaptive, structure-aware builders.<br/><br/>Ultimately, we aim to make learned indexes practically deployable by making theoretically efficient indexing structures feasible through structure-aware designs that combine data structures with learning and algorithmic techniques while preserving learned indexes’ space–time advantages in real systems.
