                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">18</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Adaptive load balancing in DAG-based consensus protocols</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po18')">Toggle Abstract</a> Zhen Ping Khor (University of Pennsylvania)*;
Mohammad Amiri (Stony Brook University)
Boon Thau Loo (University of Pennsylvania);
<br />
          <div id="po18" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
          Byzantine fault-tolerant (BFT) protocols are essential for maintaining consistency in large-scale distributed systems operating within untrustworthy environments. While traditional leader-based consensus protocols provide necessary security, they often suffer from significant performance bottlenecks due to the tight coupling of data dissemination with consensus and the sequential nature of transaction processing. To address these limitations, DAG-based consensus protocols have emerged, leveraging parallelism and decoupling block certification from total ordering to achieve higher throughput. However, despite these advancements, DAG-based systems experience notable performance degradation in asymmetric configurations, where heterogeneous hardware resources or uneven transaction loads cause slower nodes to throttle overall system growth.<br/><br/>This work addresses the critical challenge of load imbalance in DAG-based BFT systems, specifically focusing on optimizing intra-node performance. We propose an architecture that integrates an execution engine directly into each worker thread, reducing bandwidth consumption by enabling local request execution. To mitigate the load imbalances inherent in binding clients to specific workers, we introduce a novel routing mechanism combined with round-robin submission. This allows clients to submit requests to any worker while preserving total ordering, effectively balancing workload dissemination. Furthermore, we employ hypergraph partitioning to address skew within the execution engines. Our evaluation demonstrates that these mechanisms successfully mitigate internal bottlenecks, with future work planned to extend these optimization strategies to inter-node load balancing.
            </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">19</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Adaptive data stream processing on hybrid clouds</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po19')">Toggle Abstract</a> Yuanli Wang (Boston University)*; 
Prateek Jain (Boston University ); 
Lei Huang (Boston University ); 
Le Xu (University of Edinburgh); 
Vasiliki Kalavri (Boston University )
<br />
          <div id="po19" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
            Dataflow streaming systems, such as Apache Flink and Spark Streaming, are integral to modern business analytics, providing real‐time insights across industries. In this paper, we explore how these datacenter‐centric platforms can be extended to support emerging hybrid analytics pipelines that ingest data at the edge to drive applications hosted in the cloud. Our goal is to maintain full transparency for user applications, benefit from built‐in fault tolerance and out‐of‐order processing, and leverage years of open‐source ecosystem integrations. To this end, we need to overcome several challenges, as dataflow streaming engines assume homogeneous resources and lack the dynamic adaptability needed for frequent reconfiguration.<br/><br/>We introduce an adaptive data stream processing system for hybrid clouds, compatible with dataflow streaming engines. our key innovations include: (i) a \emph{query rewriter} that decomposes applications into independent, fault-tolerant segments, (ii) a dynamic \emph{routing mechanism} that allows shifting computations between edge and cloud resources without downtime, and (iii) a dynamic \emph{routing policy} that provides continuous adaptability to variable operating conditions and workloads. We implement the system on Apache Flink and evaluate it on a physical testbed with Raspberry Pi devices, as well as on a large simulated testbed of 100 VMs. Across five real-world applications, our results show that our system seamlessly shifts computations between edge and cloud, while consistently achieving higher throughput than baseline systems.
          </div>
        </td>
      </tr>
    
                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">20</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Meeting Latency SLOs on Cloud Data Warehouses</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po20')">Toggle Abstract</a> Markos Markakis (Massachusetts Institute of Technology)*; 
Ziniu Wu (Massachusetts Institute of Technology); 
Tim Kraska (Massachusetts Institute of Technology)
<br />
          <div id="po20" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
          As the cloud-based data warehousing market matures, low technical configuration requirements are emerging as an important selling point. Where customers once had to carefully provision VMs, today's "serverless" model abstracts away most of the hardware details. In the next phase of this evolution, vendors are examining cost/performance constraints as configuration inputs, moving away from explicit mentions of hardware at all. In one formulation, they aim to deliver the best performance under some dollar budget. In the formulation we focus on, one aims for acceptable latency as cheaply as possible, where "acceptable" is defined as queries meeting their latency Service-Level Objectives (SLOs). In this work, we present a framework for assembling and operating multi-compute-instance cloud-based data warehouses under a latency SLO. We discuss the opportunities afforded by recent ML advances in the field, and describe promising paths forward and their early results. This is ongoing work.
           </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">21</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Continuous Prompts: LLM-Augmented Pipeline Processing over Unstructured Streams</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po21')">Toggle Abstract</a> Shu Chen (Brown University )*; 
Deepti Raghavan (Brown University ); 
Ugur Cetintemel (Brown University ); 
<br />
          <div id="po21" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
         Monitoring unstructured data streams increasingly requires persistent, semantics-aware computation, yet today’s LLM frameworks remain largely stateless and one-shot, limiting their applicability to long-running analytics. Emerging applications—such as clinical monitoring, financial surveillance, and misinformation detection—demand continuous interpretation over evolving streams while explicitly managing performance–accuracy trade-offs under resource constraints.<br/><br/>We introduce Continuous Prompts (CPs), a new abstraction that lifts LLM-based processing and reasoning into stateful, long-lived, and adaptive stream processing, analogous to how continuous queries extend relational operators to unbounded data. CPs define continuous semantic operators with explicit state and composable semantics, enabling persistent LLM-powered pipelines that adapt to semantic drift and workload dynamics.<br/><br/>We realize CPs in VectraFlow, an LLM-augmented stream processing system that extends classical operators (e.g., filter, aggregate, top-k) to unstructured streams and introduces streaming-native semantics, including semantic windows, dynamic semantic group-by, and continuous RAG with long-lived retrieval state. To address the high cost of LLM inference, VectraFlow employs tuple batching and operator fusion, exposing inherent throughput–accuracy trade-offs. To navigate these trade-offs at runtime, we design a dynamic optimization framework that uses lightweight cost and accuracy models, online telemetry, and shadow execution to adapt execution plans. Experiments show that VectraFlow efficiently sustains continuous semantic queries, adapting to workload changes while balancing accuracy and throughput. <br/><br/>This work builds on our prior VectraFlow paper at CIDR 2025 and an extended version currently submitted to PVLDB 2026; the full paper is available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.03389.
          </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

           <tr>
              <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">22</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Eliminating the Hidden Cost of Zone Management in ZNS SSDs</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po22')">Toggle Abstract</a> Teona Bagashvili (Boston University)*; 
Tarikul Islam Papon (University of Massachusetts Boston); 
Subhadeep Sarkar (Brandeis University); 
Manos Athanassoulis (Boston University)
<br />
          <div id="po22" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
         Zoned Namespace (ZNS) SSDs represent a paradigm shift in flash-based drives, transferring the responsibility of space management from the device to the host. ZNS introduces a zoned interface, where the host writes data to the zone sequentially. The sequential write requirement aligns with the physical characteristics of SSDs, where flash pages cannot be updated in place. As a result, ZNS eliminates the need for a complex flash translation layer (FTL) and shifts responsibility for garbage collection to the host, providing finer control over data management.<br/>The ZNS standard describes how zones should be managed on the host side, however, the implementation of zone allocation and management within the controller is left to device manufacturers. In this work, we identify that the key challenge in zone allocation is balancing throughput, device-level write amplification, and controller resources such as memory and allocation latency. We identify three fundamental design dimensions that shape this tradeoff: (i) zone allocation granularity, which defines the storage elements used to construct a zone (ii) zone geometry, which determines zone size and how zones are mapped across parallel units to exploit device-level parallelism; and (iii) zone reclamation strategies that govern how partially filled zones are finished and reset. Specifically, we find that state-of-the-art zone allocation and reclamation strategies exhibit high<br/>device-level write amplification, leading to unnecessary device wear and limiting its lifetime. To address this, we propose SilentZNS, a device-level zone management strategy that allocates resources to the logical zone on demand. SilentZNS (i) represents storage as a set of storage elements (erase blocks or collections of erase blocks), (ii) allows configuring the zone geometry, and (iii) issues dummy writes only to storage elements that are (partially) written. Blocks that remain empty are reassigned to subsequent logical zones, eliminating the need for device-issued dummy writes across the entire zone.<br/>With SilentZNS, we evaluate the full spectrum of physical-zone allocation strategies. We evaluate the impact of storage element granularity, ranging from fine-grained block-level allocation, in which zones are composed of individual erase blocks, to coarse-grained full-zone allocation, in which zones are mapped to fixed sets of blocks. By varying the zone geometry and the granularity of storage elements across the two ends of the spectrum, we explore the trade-off among allocation time, write amplification, and throughput. We show that, depending on the desired properties of the zones (e.g., as determined by the applications), different allocation strategies are better suited.
          </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">23</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Bringing Vector Search to OLTP</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po23')">Toggle Abstract</a> Shu Zhou (Google)*
<br />
          <div id="po23" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
            This poster presents the design and implementation of a fully-transactional and highly-performant Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) vector search feature in Google Cloud SQL for MySQL. We address the fundamental challenges of integrating an in-memory ANN library, Scalable Nearest Neighbors (ScaNN), into an ACID-compliant, multi-version concurrency control (MVCC) environment, delivering a solution that is both powerful and seamlessly integrated for MySQL users.
            </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">24</td>
        <td>
          <strong>RAGged Results: Diagnosing Vector Index Selection in Retrieval-Augmented Generation</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po24')">Toggle Abstract</a> Zikai Wang (Northeastern University)*
<br />
          <div id="po24" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
           Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as a dominant paradigm for enhancing large language models with external knowledge. While recent research has focused on improving RAG algorithms, an often overlooked yet critical component is the underlying vector index—whose choice significantly influences the ultimate RAG quality—but is frequently treated as a mere implementation detail. We observe that RAG developers are typically unaware of how index selection impacts end-to-end behavior; existing evaluation practices, such as average recall, insufficiently capture the effects of index design on RAG performance; and index selection remains ad hoc, guided more by convenience than by systematic analysis.<br/>This paper quantifies the impact of vector index choices on RAG performance, introduces a new metric for evaluating index quality, and presents a selection framework to identify the most effective indexes among available options. Our results show that deliberate index selection is critical for building reliable RAG systems, improving end-to-end performance by up to five percentage points (e.g., 69%→74% in Q&amp;A RAG).
             </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">25</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Split Layouts</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po25')">Toggle Abstract</a> Andrew Briasco-Stewart (Boston University)*; 
Konstantinos Karatsenidis (Boston University); 
Manos Athanassoulis (Boston University); 
Renato Mancuso (Boston University)
<br />
          <div id="po25" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
            Analytical data systems typically rely on a single physical data layout, where data is stored column-first. While column-stores avoid accessing unwanted fields, they suffer from the cost of tuple reconstruction, which is higher as more columns participate in the query. On the other hand, row-stores lead to a lot of unnecessary data movement for most analytical queries. On-the-fly data transformation supported by Relational Memory (RME) leverages custom hardware to offer the ideal layout per query, reducing cache line transfers. However, RME may still incur unnecessary accesses when fetching projected fields of rows that are eventually filtered out.<br/>We propose several novel hybrid data layouts to address these challenges: a split row-store structure that separates projection and selection columns, a hybrid column-row layout that combines the strengths of column-stores for selection columns and row-stores for projection columns, iterations of the two with an additional RME engine for columns that are used to join two tables together, and a possible another RME engine for columns used in both selection and projection. These layouts are designed to optimize memory access patterns for analytical queries by reducing cache line transfers and improving memory bandwidth efficiency. We demonstrate that the proposed layouts significantly outperform traditional row-wise, columnar, and RME-based layouts in terms of execution time and memory efficiency, leading to up to 1.55 times query speedup by reducing data movement up to 65%. Our experimental results show that the best performance can be achieved when selection columns are stored in a columnar fashion and projected columns in a row-wise manner. We note that this would typically mean a different layout for each query, which is why this work advocates for extending prior art on Relational Memory to make this feasible.
            </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">26</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Evaluating Filters Augmented with Adaptation vs ML Models</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po26')">Toggle Abstract</a> Diandre Miguel Sabale (Northeastern University)*; 
Prashant Pandey (Northeastern University)
<br />
          <div id="po26" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
         Learned filters and adaptive filters both represent promising advances over traditional filters for approximate membership testing, and are both considered state-of-the-art filter design spaces. Learned filters improve space-accuracy trade-off by leveraging machine learning models trained on the dataset to enhance their decision-making. Meanwhile, adaptive filters dynamically update their representation when false positives occur, ensuring bounded false positive rates independent of query distribution.<br/><br/>While these two filter paradigms have demonstrated strong empirical performances when compared against traditional baselines, their evaluations usually exclude each other–no prior work has considered directly comparing the two in practical workloads. This evaluation gap leaves system designers without guidance for selecting the appropriate filter type.<br/><br/>This project offers the first comparative evaluation of the two paradigms using real-world datasets and diverse query distributions seen in prior benchmarks and database settings.<br/><br/>We demonstrate that across all workloads, even those which learned filters are optimized for, adaptive filters exhibit competitive space efficiencies. However, in cases with skewed or dynamic workloads, adaptive filters demonstrate orders of magnitudes lower false positive rates. Additionally, we find that model training and inference are an additional overhead on standard filter operations which make them orders of magnitude slower than adaptive (and traditional) filters, making them impractical in many throughput-dependent systems applications.<br/><br/>Based on our analysis, learned filters are most suitable for stable and highly predictable workloads where space efficiency is paramount, while adaptive filters are a more generalizable option which can handle dynamic environments.
           </div>
        </td>
      </tr>


                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">27</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Jasper: An Updatable and Scalable GPU ANNS System</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po27')">Toggle Abstract</a> Hunter McCoy (Northeastern University)*; 
Zikun Wang (Northeastern University); 
Prashant Pandey (Northeastern University)
<br />
          <div id="po27" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
        Approximate nearest neighbor search (ANNS) is fundamental to modern machine learning and information retrieval applications, from recommendation systems to retrieval-augmented generation. Graph-based indices have emerged as the state-of-the-art approach, offering superior recall-throughput tradeoffs through navigable search graphs that are traversed greedily to locate nearest neighbors. While GPUs offer massive parallelism that can accelerate both index construction and querying, existing GPU ANNS systems face significant challenges: unstructured memory access patterns degrade performance, high-dimensional vectors strain memory bandwidth, and most critically, current solutions lack support for dynamic updates—a requirement for real-world streaming workloads.<br/><br/>We present Jasper, a GPU-native ANNS system built on the Vamana graph index that achieves both high performance and full updatability. Our approach addresses GPU memory optimization holistically through three key contributions:<br/><br/>1. a GPU-efficient implementation of \RQ quantization that reduces memory footprint up to 8x without the random access penalties of product Quantization.<br/>2. A tile-based loading scheme that issues blocked 16-byte loads<br/>to minimize latency while maintaining peak throughput.<br/>3. A batch-parallel construction algorithm adapted for CUDA that enables lock-free streaming insertions.<br/><br/>Our evaluation across five standard benchmarks shows that Jasper achieves up to 1.93x higher query throughput than CAGRA, the current state-of-the-art GPU index, while providing updatability that CAGRA lacks. Compared to BANG, the previous fastest GPU Vamana implementation, Jasper delivers 19–131x faster queries. Jasper has the fastest construction time of any ANNS index, outperforming the state of the art by up to 4.1x. Jasper's incremental construction degrades gracefully, with a 20x increase in index size reducing insertion throughput by less than 50\%, making it suitable for production streaming workloads.<br/><br/>We are submitting as a talk and would also like to submit a poster.
            </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <!-- <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Evergreen: Efficient Claim Verification for Semantic Aggregates</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po28')">Toggle Abstract</a> Alexander Lee (Brown University and Snowflake Inc.)*;
Benjamin Han (Snowflake Inc.); 
Shayak Sen (Snowflake Inc.); 
Sam Yeom (Snowflake Inc.); 
Ugur Cetintemel (Brown University and Snowflake Inc.); 
Anupam Datta (Snowflake Inc.)
<br />
          <div id="po28" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
      As Large Language Models (LLMs) become integral to modern analytics, users increasingly rely on semantic aggregates such as natural language summaries and comparative analyses. These aggregates, however, implicitly assert claims that may not be verified.<br/><br/>In this talk, we introduce Evergreen, a system to efficiently verify LLM-generated claims against facts present in the input tuples used to produce semantic aggregates. Evergreen decomposes natural language responses into atomic claims and compiles them into verification queries composed of structured and semantic operators. Its execution engine returns binary verification results together with provenance, indicating how each outcome depends on facts present in the input tuples. We also present a suite of system-level optimizations—including early termination, semantic sorting, operator fusion, sampling, and cross-claim caching—that substantially reduce verification cost.<br/><br/>Using a real-world restaurant reviews dataset, we demonstrate that Evergreen can verify a wide range of claims at significantly lower cost than naïve end-to-end LLM evaluation. These preliminary results suggest that claim-aware query planning and optimization make verification practical at scale. We conclude by outlining future directions in table grounding, including the integration of our techniques into emerging AI-SQL systems. By treating natural language claim verification as a first-class query processing problem, Evergreen enables reliable and efficient evaluation for next-generation AI-augmented data systems.
            </div>
        </td>
      </tr> -->

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">29</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Streaming GNN Inference on dynamic graphs</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po29')">Toggle Abstract</a> Naima Abrar Shami (Boston University)*;
Yuhang Song (Boston University); 
Sakshi Sharma (Boston University); 
Lei Huang (Boston University); 
Vasiliki Kalavri (Boston University)
<br />
          <div id="po29" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
     Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) are effective for tasks such as fraud detection, recommendation, and traffic analysis, where predictions depend on relationships between entities. However, most existing GNN inference pipelines assume static graphs and batch processing, making them unsuitable for graphs that evolve continuously over time. We present a streaming GNN inference system that enables real-time inference directly on dynamic graphs by continuously incorporating new updates and extracting local neighborhoods on demand. Our system provides a simple, declarative interface to configure neighborhood size and sampling behavior, while handling the underlying distributed execution automatically. This allows GNNs to be applied to real-time, streaming applications.
            </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <!-- <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>KramaBench: A Benchmark for AI Systems on Data-Intensive Tasks</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po30')">Toggle Abstract</a> Gerardo Vitagliano (MIT CSAIL)*; 
Eugenie Lai (MIT CSAIL); 
Ziyu Zhang (MIT CSAIL); 
Om Chabra (MIT CSAIL); 
Michael Cafarella (MIT CSAIL); 
Sam Madden (MIT CSAIL); 
Tim Kraska (MIT CSAIL)
<br />
          <div id="po30" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
 Real-world data science requires navigating complex pipelines across heterogeneous, unclean data—yet current AI benchmarks focus primarily on isolated tasks like code generation or text-to-SQL. We present KramaBench, a comprehensive benchmark evaluating end-to-end data science capabilities of LLM-based systems through 104 manually curated challenges spanning 1,700 files across 6 domains (archaeology, astronomy, biomedical research, environmental science, legal discovery, and wildfire analysis).<br/>Our goal is to provide fine-grained insights into where and why systems fail when solving data to insights tasks. To ensure our benchmark reflects realistic pipelines, each of the benchmark tasks is grounded in real-world datasets that have been the<br/>subject of scientific studies and publicly available reports. <br/>Our evaluation includes 6 different agentic frameworks which we test with 8 different LLM backends. We include both single agent baselines (with different degrees of data access) as well as state-of-the-art multi-agent baselines. Overall, our results demonstrate that while multi-agent baselines are capable of outperforming prompt-based and single agent baselines, current LLM systems remain far from solving real-world data science problems, highlighting urgent research needs in data-<br/>dependent reasoning, robust domain knowledge integration, and holistic data lake understanding.
    </div>
        </td>
      </tr> -->

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">31</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Low-Overhead Adaptive Execution via Reinforcement Learning with Bootstrapped Counterfactuals</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po31')">Toggle Abstract</a> Zijie Zhao (University of Pennsylvania)*; 
Ryan Marcus (University of Pennsylvania)
<br />
          <div id="po31" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
 Modern data systems rely heavily on low-level kernels for basic operations such as filtering and aggregation. Pull-based execution engines that employ morsel-driven parallelism provide substantially more opportunities to optimize kernel selection: by partitioning input data into many small, independent chunks (morsels), the system enables each morsel to execute a sequence of short, self-contained operations—such as predicate evaluation or hash computation—using different implementation variants (e.g., scalar versus vectorized execution). The combination of these choices can have a significant impact on end-to-end performance; however, existing systems typically adopt fixed, worst-case–oriented implementations, leaving much of the optimization potential arising from hardware, query, and data characteristics unexplored.<br/><br/>This paper proposes a low-overhead adaptive execution engine based on reinforcement learning with bootstrapped counterfactuals. The key insight is that, at the morsel granularity, the system can selectively execute alternative kernel implementations on a small subset of morsels to generate counterfactual performance samples. We use these samples to build a bootstrapped Nadaraya–Watson kernel regression model that estimates the performance of candidate implementations and derives empirical confidence intervals to determine when a statistically significant optimal choice can be made. Exploration is guided by the outcomes of these tests. Experiments on the IMDB and StackOverflow datasets demonstrate that the proposed approach achieves near-optimal performance.
              </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <!-- <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Optimizing Semantic Top-k Operators with Listwise Prompting</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po32')">Toggle Abstract</a> Jason Shin (University of Rochester); 
Jiwon Chang (University of Rochester); 
Fatemeh Nargesian (University of Rochester)*
<br />
          <div id="po32" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
A long-standing goal of data management systems has been to deliver quantitative insights on a large scale. Today, the semantic capabilities
of large language models (LLMs) enable AI-driven analytics systems to reason over massive volumes of both structured and unstructured
data. Supporting such AI-powered analytical queries requires systems that seamlessly combine query processing with user-defined queries.
Achieving full-fledged analytics, with efficient, accurate, and general-purpose query support, would be a transformative breakthrough.
<br />
        </td>
      </tr> -->

                <!-- <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Bringing Vector Search to OLTP</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po33')">Toggle Abstract</a> Shu Zhou (Google)*
<br />
          <div id="po33" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
This talk discusses the design and implementation of a fully-transactional and highly-performant Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) vector search feature in Google Cloud SQL for MySQL. We address the fundamental challenges of integrating an in-memory ANN library, Scalable Nearest Neighbors (ScaNN), into an ACID-compliant, multi-version concurrency control (MVCC) environment, delivering a solution that is both powerful and seamlessly integrated for MySQL users.
          </div>
