                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">34</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Fishing in Deeper Waters: A Comprehensive MEV Analysis of DAG-Based Blockchains</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po34')">Toggle Abstract</a> Iliya Mirzaei (Stony Brook University)*; 
Mohammad Javad Amiri (Stony Brook University)
<br />
          <div id="po34" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
As distributed data management systems increasingly operate in untrusted environments, adversarial transaction ordering has become a major concern due to the absence of trusted intermediaries. Transaction proposers can exploit transaction inclusion and ordering to extract maximal extractable value (MEV), a threat not inherently addressed by Byzantine Fault-Tolerant consensus protocols despite their safety and liveness guarantees. While order-fairness mechanisms aim to mitigate such manipulation, they often incur high performance overhead, motivating the development of high-performance BFT protocols that allow multiple nodes to propose blocks concurrently. This design forms Directed Acyclic Graphs of blocks and underpins modern DAG-based BFT protocols such as DAG-Rider, Narwhal and Tusk, Bullshark, Shoal++, Autobahn, and Shardag.<br/>Ensuring order fairness in DAG-based systems is challenging. While concurrent block proposals improve throughput, the flexibility of DAG structures enables complex inter-block relationships that adversaries can exploit to manipulate transaction ordering, such as placing their blocks ahead of victims’. Without strong order-fairness guarantees, DAGs’ performance benefits may be undermined by these attacks.<br/>Recent work shows that DAG-based systems are vulnerable to MEV and frontrunning attacks, but a systematic analysis across modern DAG protocols is lacking. This study addresses this gap by quantifying extraction opportunities across six major protocols. We implement and evaluate three frontrunning strategies—Fissure, Speculative, and Sluggish—on Bullshark, Mysticeti, Mahi-Mahi, Autobahn, AlephBFT, and Narwhal Tusk and their popular open-source forks. By measuring attack success rates across this broader set, we uncover architectural patterns not identified in prior work.<br/>Our findings expose a wide disparity in MEV opportunities. Protocols like Bullshark are highly vulnerable, with attackers achieving nearly 90% success rates in frontrunning victim transactions. In contrast, AlephBFT and Mahi-Mahi present exploitable but limited opportunities (52% to 86% ASR), constrained by specific voting or timing mechanisms. Finally, Autobahn and Mysticeti demonstrate remarkable resilience (∼50% ASR), effectively neutralizing the tested extraction strategies. We further analyze how underlying architectural features, such as single-layer vs. multi layer consensus and virtual voting, enable or mitigate these extraction opportunities. Ongoing work extends this analysis to larger-scale networks to evaluate the impact of latency and geo-distribution on MEV feasibility.
             </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">35</td>
        <td>
          <strong>QuART: Quick Ingestion to ART through Sortedness Awareness</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po35')">Toggle Abstract</a> Can Gokmen (Boston University)*; 
Aneesh Raman (Boston University); 
Manos Athanassoulis (Boston University)
<br />
          <div id="po35" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Database indexes are a fundamental building block of data systems, enabling fast point lookups and range queries in OLTP engines, key-value stores, and streaming platforms such as Redis, which is backed by a radix tree implementation. In modern ingestion-heavy workloads, keys like timestamps or monotonically increasing IDs typically arrive in-order or close to being in-order (near-sorted). However, modern radix tree implementations, like the Adaptive Radix Tree (ART), traverse from the root on every insert, repeating almost the same path even when the workload is highly sorted. This results in suboptimal performance, especially for near-sorted data. Prior work on tree indexes for near-sorted data has explored fast path optimizations that avoid redundant tree traversals, namely tail-leaf (tail), last-inserted-leaf (lil), and predicted ordered leaf (pole). These optimizations, however, have not been explored in conjunction with radix trees, but rather only with B+-Tree variants.<br/><br/>To address this gap, we propose Quick Ingestion ART (QuART), introducing fast-path insertion into the Adaptive Radix Tree. QuART supports three fast-path optimizations: tail and lil that are adapted to the radix tree setting, and stail, a robust fast-path tailored for radix trees. tail reuses a fast-path pointer when the new key follows the previous one and otherwise falls back to a normal root insert, whereas lil uses the same fast-path vs. root decision as tail, but eagerly updates the fast-path metadata on every insert. In the radix setting, these optimizations offered limited benefits with varying degrees of sortedness. On the other hand, stail maintains a fast path pointer for recent inserts but updates it only on carefully chosen bridge keys drawn from a small neighborhood of the current fast-path pointer (e.g., when the new key belongs to a new radix partition), using structural properties of the radix tree to distinguish in-order from out-of-order entries. This yields a constant and very small number of fast path updates (for 32 byte integers, N/256, where N is the number of keys), which avoids the thrashing behavior that eager schemes (like lil) suffer under disorder. Across differently sorted workloads generated with the Benchmark on Data Sortedness (BoDS) spanning the full spectrum of K–L values (where K measures how many elements are out-of-order in a data collection and L measures by how much the out-of-order entries are displaced from their respective in-order positions), stail improves insertion throughput over baseline ART by up to 55% and maintains a clear advantage whenever the workload exhibits any nonzero sortedness. <br/><br/>The fast-paths proposed by QuART exploit sortedness to avoid unnecessary tree traversals without changing the tree structure, interface, or lookup semantics, making it straightforward to adopt in existing industrial codebases. This makes QuART a high-performance drop-in replacement for any storage engine that uses ART-style radix trees.
            </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <!-- <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Understanding Human Judgment in Table Unionability</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po36')">Toggle Abstract</a> Nina Klimenkova (Worcester Polytechnic Institute)*; 
Roee Shraga (Worcester Polytechnic Institute); 
Sreeram Marimuthu (Worcester Polytechnic Institute)
<br />
          <div id="po36" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
In this talk, Nina Klimenkova, one of the first authors, will present a study that examines how humans reason about table unionability through a controlled experimental survey and behavioral analysis. The findings are used to develop a machine learning framework that improves raw human judgments and to study how large language models (LLMs) behave under different levels of human context. This talk is based on the ACM SIGMOD 2025 HILDA workshop paper Humans, Machine Learning, and Language Models in Union: A Cognitive Study on Table Unionability.
            </div>
        </td>
      </tr> -->


                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">37</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Flow-centric Query Evaluation Pipelines</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po37')">Toggle Abstract</a> Victoria Dib (University at Buffalo, SUNY); 
Andrew Mikalsen (University at Buffalo, SUNY); 
Krishna Sivakumar (University at Buffalo, SUNY); 
Jaroslaw Zola (University at Buffalo, SUNY); 
Oliver Kennedy (University at Buffalo, SUNY)*
<br />
          <div id="po37" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Most database engines evaluate queries by compiling them down to an execution pipeline of composable operators that each encapsulate part of the query’s compute and/or IO. Operators are chained together through an abstraction of relational data streams, typically framed as push- or pull-based. This streaming abstraction fundamentally pushes state management into operators. Unfortunately, in a typical out-of-core database system, state management consumes limited shared resources like memory and IO. Globally managing how such resources are shared requires poking holes through the simple stream-based operator abstraction. In this talk, we will outline our work-in-progress “dataﬂow-oriented” pipeline abstraction that permits such optimizations without breaking the abstraction boundaries.
             </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <!-- <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Magneto: Combining Small and Large Language Models for Schema Matching</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po38')">Toggle Abstract</a> Yurong Liu (New York University)*; 
Eduardo Pena (Federal University of Technology Paraná); 
Aécio Santos (New York University); 
Eden Wu (New York University); 
Juliana Freire (New York University)
<br />
          <div id="po38" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Recent advances in language models opened new opportunities to address complex schema matching tasks. Schema matching approaches have been proposed that demonstrate the usefulness of language models, but they have also uncovered important limitations: Small language models (SLMs) require training data (which can be both expensive and challenging to obtain), and large language models (LLMs) often incur high computational costs and must deal with constraints imposed by context windows. We present Magneto, a cost-effective and accurate solution for schema matching that combines the advantages of SLMs and LLMs to address their limitations. By structuring the schema matching pipeline in two phases, retrieval and reranking, Magneto can use computationally efficient SLM-based strategies to derive candidate matches which can then be reranked by LLMs, thus making it possible to reduce runtime without compromising matching accuracy. We propose a self-supervised approach to fine-tune SLMs which uses LLMs to generate syntactically diverse training data, and prompting strategies that are effective for reranking. We also introduce a new benchmark, developed in collaboration with domain experts, which includes real biomedical datasets and presents new challenges to schema matching methods. Through a detailed experimental evaluation, using both our new and existing benchmarks, we show that Magneto is scalable and attains high accuracy for datasets from different domains.
               </div>
        </td>
      </tr> -->

                <!-- <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Diva: Dynamic Range Filter for Var-Length Keys and Queries</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po39')">Toggle Abstract</a> Navid Eslami (University of Toronto ); 
Ioana Bercea (KTH Royal Institute of Technology); 
Niv Dayan (University of Toronto)*
<br />
          <div id="po39" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
We propose to present our VLDB 2025 paper, ``Diva: Dynamic Range Filter for Variable-Length Keys and Queries.'' The paper received the 2025 VLDB Best Paper Award and was recently invited for the 2026 SIGMOD Record Research Highlights. The talk will be given by Niv Dayan, who was a postdoctoral researcher at Harvard University from 2015 to 2019, and is therefore an alumnus of the Northeastern Database Community. Niv is currently an assistant professor at the University of Toronto. <br/><br/>Range filters are compact probabilistic data structures that answer approximate range emptiness queries. They are used in many domains, e.g., in key-value stores, to quickly rule out the existence of keys in a given query range and avoid having to search for them in storage. However, all existing range filters exhibit at least one of the following shortcomings: (1)~ they do not provide robust false positive rate and performance guarantees, (2)~they do not support variable-length keys and query ranges, and (3)~they do not allow dynamic operations such as insertions, deletions, or expansions.<br/><br/>We introduce Diva, the first range filter to address all the above challenges simultaneously. Diva learns the dataset's distribution by sampling keys and storing them in a cache-efficient trie. It compresses the keys in-between samples by removing their longest common prefix and truncating their suffixes while leaving enough bits in the middle (i.e., an infix) to allow differentiating between the keys in the sorted order. It stores infixes in constant time dynamic data blocks, which it splits to handle insertions and expansions. It processes a range query by traversing the trie and checking for the inclusion of infixes in the target query range. We show, theoretically and empirically, that Diva achieves a false positive rate on par with the state of the art on real-world datasets while supporting dynamicity and variable-length queries and keys.
            </div>
        </td>
      </tr> -->

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">40</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Stochastic Variational Inference for Relational Probabilistic Programming</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po40')">Toggle Abstract</a> Sami Hadouaj (University of Michigan-Dearborn)*; 
Ouael Ben Amara (University of Michigan-Dearborn); 
Niccolo Meneghetti ( University of Michigan-Dearborn)
<br />
          <div id="po40" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Traditional database systems are highly effective at managing structured data, but they lack native support for reasoning under uncertainty and for modeling complex generative AI models. Although probabilistic extensions of Datalog have been proposed [1] to mitigate these limitations, they generally do not support inference, which remains an open research challenge, particularly at scale.<br/>A probabilistic Datalog program typically combines a formal specification of a stochastic tuple-generating process with a set of logical constraints that restrict the space of admissible outcomes. The key challenge is to efficiently approximate the resulting posterior distribution.<br/>In this paper, we bridge the gap between declarative data modeling and scalable probabilistic reasoning by introducing a fully automated stochastic variational inference pipeline within the StarfishDB [2] ecosystem. Our approach transforms the inference problem into an optimization task: starting from a generative Datalog theory, our system automatically derives a suitable family of variational distributions and synthesizes an optimization routine to minimize the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence. To maximize performance, our method employs knowledge compilation techniques to limit the number<br/>of variational parameters, without compromising the representational power of the variational surrogate. Empirical evaluations show that our approach provides a fast-converging alternative that rivals specialized, model-specific algorithms. This allows databases to natively perform sophisticated probabilistic reasoning, providing a seamless declarative path from standard data management to complex logical probabilistic programming.<br/><br/><br/><br/>[1] Vince BáRány, Balder Ten Cate, Benny Kimelfeld, Dan Olteanu, and Zografoula Vagena. 2017. Declarative Probabilistic Programming with Datalog. ACM Trans. Database Syst. 42, 4, Article 22 (December 2017), 35 pages. https://doi.org/10.1145/3132700<br/><br/><br/>[2] Ouael Ben Amara, Sami Hadouaj, and Niccolò Meneghetti. 2024. StarfishDB: A Query Execution Engine for Relational Probabilistic Programming. Proc. ACM Manag. Data 2, 3, Article 185 (June 2024), 31 pages. https://doi.org/10.1145/3654988<br/>
            </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">41</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Log Anomaly Detection via Semantic Hierarchy</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po41')">Toggle Abstract</a> Lei Ma (WPI)*; 
Elke Rundensteiner (WPI); 
Dennis Hofmann (WPI)
<br />
          <div id="po41" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
In this talk, first author Lei Ma will show how the effectiveness and efficiency of log anomaly detection can be<br/>both improved by exploiting the hidden semantic structure of log data. This talk is based on the paper ”KRONE:<br/>Hierarchical Log Anomaly Detection” under revision of ICDE 26.
        </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">42</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Benchmarking Tabular Representation Models on Longitudinal Data </strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po42')">Toggle Abstract</a> PRATIK POKHAREL (University at Buffalo)*; 
Oliver Kennedy (University at Buffalo)
<br />
          <div id="po42" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
In this talk, we will discuss our efforts to develop a data integration solution for social science researchers conducting longitudinal<br/>studies. We will present a comparative study of the effectiveness of state-of-the-art open tabular representation models for table union<br/>search workload over a longitudinal survey dataset. While classical and longitudinal data integration share fundamental challenges,<br/>existing tabular representation models for open datasets implicitly assume that semantics are grounded in cell values and external<br/>entities, such as knowledge bases or distributional semantics from pretrained language models. Longitudinal survey datasets violate<br/>this assumption: semantics are defined by measurement instruments and their latent constructs, rather than solely by the data they<br/>produce. Additionally, the incremental and structured nature of longitudinal studies makes them a unique case for data integration. In<br/>particular, attributes are derived from prose questions rather than semantically rich identifiers, and these questions frequently co-refer.<br/>We outline an approach that incorporates these characteristics into column representation models.
          </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">43</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Modeling Perception in Sampling for Visualizations </strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po43')">Toggle Abstract</a> Redwan Ahmed Rizvee (University of Massachusetts Amherst)*; 
Zafeiria Moumoulidou (Meta Inc.); 
Alexandra Meliou (University of Massachusetts Amherst)
<br />
          <div id="po43" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Visualization is a crucial component of data analytics workflows, but massive datasets present challenges in terms of computational complexity and visual clutter. To address this, analysts often downsample data; yet, finding representative samples that preserve the visual characteristics of the complete data remains difficult. A shortcoming of standard methods is that they do not directly account for an important factor: how humans perceive a visualization (e.g., identification of trends, clusters, outliers, and other patterns). Perception-Aware Sampling (PAwS) is a novel method that aims to optimize samples for human perception. It achieves this by using saliency maps, a standard tool in computer vision, as a proxy for perception. These maps form the basis of deriving perception scores for the data, and these scores are subsequently used in sampling. While PAwS produces samples that better preserve visual fidelity, it has a serious computational bottleneck: Deriving perception scores through saliency requires first visualizing the original data, which is arguably wasteful in this context, and invoking a saliency model over the scatterplot is an expensive operation. We propose a new method to model perception, based on standard density estimators, which entirely forgoes the expensive saliency invocation. PAwS^d, our density-driven version of PAwS, can achieve results of similar perceptual quality as PAwS, while reducing pre-processing costs by about 90%.
            </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">44</td>
        <td>
          <strong>FluidLSM: A Fluid Log-Structured Merge Tree for Shifting Workloads</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po44')">Toggle Abstract</a> Shubham Kaushik (Brandeis University)*; 
Steven Yang (Brandeis University); 
Subhadeep Sarkar (Brandeis University)
<br />
          <div id="po44" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Log-structured merge (LSM) trees are widely used in modern key–value stores to support high write throughput and competitive query performance. Different LSM-tree configurations are suited to different workload regimes: leveled LSM-trees favor read-heavy workloads, while tiered LSM-trees perform best under write-heavy workloads. However, real-world workloads frequently exhibit dynamic, diurnal, and bursty patterns, and a single static configuration cannot provide consistently optimal performance over time. Reconfiguring an LSM-tree typically requires restarting or reorganizing the storage engine, which introduces downtime, delays, and significant operational overhead. In this work, we introduce FluidLSM, an adaptive LSM-tree that continuously monitors workload characteristics and dynamically transitions between internal LSM shapes. FluidLSM adjusts parameters at each level, such as size ratios, the number of runs (for tiered levels), compaction and file-picking policies, compression settings, and write-buffer (memtable) implementation and sizes, at runtime without requiring system restarts. By fluidly adapting to changing workload conditions, \algo{} provides more stable performance, reduces write amplification during ingestion bursts, and improves read efficiency during query-intensive periods.
             </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">45</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Tectonic: Bridging Synthetic and Real-World Workloads for Key-Value Benchmarking</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po45')">Toggle Abstract</a> Boao Chen (Brandeis University )*; 
Shubham Kaushik (Brandeis University); 
Alexander H. Ott (Brandeis university); 
Subhadeep Sarkar (Brandeis University)
<br />
          <div id="po45" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
The storage engines play a critical role in modern data systems—ranging from NoSQL systems like Rocksdb and DynamoDB to SQL systems like CockroachDB—which primarily rely on Log-Structured Merge-trees (LSM) and Key-Value stores. As these systems are deployed in a variety of emerging applications, the workloads they experience have grown increasingly complex, often showing dynamic behaviors such as operation shifts, different key distributions, and various degrees of data ingestion order. However, existing state-of-the-art key-value benchmarks, including YCSB, KVBench, and db_bench, are mostly static. They lack the feature to simulate dynamic workloads where the mix and pattern of database operations change unpredictably over time. They cannot create composite keys that feature diverse prefix distributions or generate workloads with different levels of data sortedness. These limitations lead to inaccurate performance evaluations and make it difficult to truly understand a commercial key-value store's behavior. To address these concerns, we propose Tectonic, a high-performance workload generator implemented in Rust. Tectonic is architected to model real-world data access by (i) supporting dynamic workload shifts, where operations and distributions change over time; (ii) accurate control over composite key generation; (iii) configurable data access patterns for common database operations ranging from inserts to point and range deletes; and (iv) configurable data sortedness when generating workloads. Our experiment results demonstrate that Tectonic achieves up to 2 times higher throughput and 84% reduction in memory footprint compared to state-of-the-art workload generators.
              </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <!-- <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>TurtleKV: A Dynamically Tunable Key/Value Store</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po46')">Toggle Abstract</a> Anthony Astolfi (The MathWorks)*; 
Vidya Silai (The MathWorks); 
Darby Huye (Tufts University); 
Lan Liu (Tufts University); 
Raja Sambasivan (Tufts University); 
Johes Bater (Tufts University)
<br />
          <div id="po46" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Key/Value Storage Engines, a crucial building block for modern databases and applications, are constrained by fundamental resource cost trade-offs that prevent simultaneous optimal tuning for all workloads. The basic, two-way trade-offs from which this constraint emerges are not equally advantageous as tuning knobs, however: altering the stored structure of data to target a tuning point (i.e., trading reads against writes) makes it expensive <br/>to change the tuning objective later. Memory-based trade-offs (read-memory, write-memory) offer lower retuning costs, but write-vs-memory, or WM tuning is poorly supported in current system designs. We present TurtleKV, a new key/value storage engine utilizing a novel external memory dictionary, the TurtleTree, as an alternative. TurtleKV, though its selection of primary data structure and multi-core optimized system design, enables dynamic, runtime tuning of read-memory and write-memory trade-offs to target optimal performance for changing workloads. In the Yahoo Cloud Serving Benchmark (YCSB) TurtleKV is able to match or exceed the performance of state-of-the-art storage engines SplinterDB, RocksDB, and WiredTiger.
              </div>
        </td>
      </tr> -->

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">47</td>
        <td>
          <strong>UNIZNS: Optimizing Any LSM Compactions for ZNS SSDs</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po47')">Toggle Abstract</a> Farhan S. Chowdhury (BUET); 
Shadman Saqib Eusuf (UMass Boston); 
Subhadeep Sarkar (Brandeis University); 
Tarikul Islam Papon (UMass Boston)*
<br />
          <div id="po47" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Log-structured merge (LSM) trees are used for storing large volumes of key-value pairs in persistent storage, where all key-value pairs are kept as immutable Sorted String Table (SST) files across multiple levels and periodically reorganized through compaction. The log-structured nature of the database works well with Zoned Namespace (ZNS) SSDs, where zones can be only sequentially written and must be erased as a whole, shifting responsibilities such as file placement, zone allocation, and garbage collection to the host application. Since the unit of erasure is an entire zone, zones may contain a mix of valid and invalid files, and reclaiming space through garbage collection causes write amplification when valid files must be moved. The root cause of GC-induced write amplification is the difference in the lifetime of different files placed in a zone, where file lifetime is primarily determined by the compaction policy and the nature of the workload. In this work, we focus on RocksDB and ZenFS and introduce an experimental platform named UNIZNS that integrates several existing and new file placement, garbage collection, and zone allocation policies, exposes these choices as tunable parameters, and enables a thorough experimental study to understand which policy combinations work best under different compaction policies and workloads.
              </div>
        </td>
      </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">48</td>
        <td>
          <strong>SIEVE-DB: Adapting the SIEVE Policy for DB Bufferpools</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po48')">Toggle Abstract</a> Aroma Hoque (BUET);
Tarikul Islam Papon (UMass Boston)*
<br />
          <div id="po48" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
The buffer pool is a central component of a database management system whose effectiveness directly impacts query latency, throughput, and overall system efficiency, especially under memory pressure and mixed workloads. Traditional page replacement policies such as LRU and CLOCK struggle under these conditions, often leading to cache pollution and suboptimal throughput, while the original SIEVE algorithm is not tailored to database workloads and lacks scan resistance, recency tracking, and support for write-back operations. This work presents a database-oriented variant of SIEVE named SIEVE-DB designed specifically for buffer pool management, adapting its admission and eviction mechanisms to database semantics, introducing lightweight per-page state to distinguish transient scans from sustained reuse, and supporting write-back and page pinning. Implemented in PostgreSQL 18.0, the proposed approach preserves SIEVE’s simplicity and low overhead while improving throughput by up to 6%, with even larger gains under memory pressure.
               </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">49</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Natural Language Meets Analytics: How IBM Powers Enterprise-Grade Text2SQL</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po49')">Toggle Abstract</a> Vijaya Bashyam (IBM); 
Neerju Gupta (IBM)
<br />
          <div id="po49" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
IBM’s Data Intelligence suite introduces a robust Text2SQL engine—an AI-powered natural language interface that empowers users to ask business questions in plain English and instantly receive executable SQL queries. This capability leverages watsonx models and deep semantic integration, dramatically reducing technical bottlenecks and accelerating access to data. The system is designed from the ground up for enterprise scale: it supports hybrid, multi-cloud, SaaS, and on-premises deployments, ensuring secure, governed access to data across distributed environments.<br/>Three core innovations enable this seamless experience. First, Schema Linking connects natural language phrases directly to cataloged tables and columns, grounding user intent within the governed schema. Second, Metadata Enrichment applies large language models to enhance metadata—turning cryptic names into meaningful descriptions—and employs vector embeddings to improve semantic matches. Third, Dynamic Few-Shot Prompting harnesses historical query logs and real-world examples to build context-aware prompts tailored to each user’s phrasing and enterprise schema. Together, these pillars create a fluid Text2SQL workflow that dynamically retrieves schema information, verifies logic across SQL dialects, and asks clarifying questions when needed to ensure accuracy.<br/>The result is not a proof-of-concept, but a production-ready service built into IBM’s watsonx.data intelligence platform. Users—from business analysts to engineers—can move from question to insight in seconds rather than days. The solution significantly reduces reliance on technical resources, increases confidence through standardization and auditability, and fosters a true data-driven culture.<br/>Attendees of this session will gain insight into the end-to-end architecture—including metadata ingestion, vectorization pipelines, prompt generation, and governance integration. They will also learn best practices for preparing and enriching metadata, along with strategies for governing conversational interfaces enterprise-wide. This talk provides both technical depth and actionable guidance for organizations aiming to democratize data access and operationalize natural-language querying at scale.
              </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

               <tr>
                  <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">50</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Learning from the Unseen: Unsupervised Anomaly Generation for Improved Anomaly Detection</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po50')">Toggle Abstract</a> Dennis Hofmann (Worcester Polytechnic Institute)*; 
Joshua DeOliveira (Worcester Polytechnic Institute); 
Peter VanNostrand (Worcester Polytechnic Institute); 
Lei Ma (Worcester Polytechnic Institute); 
Lei Cao (University of Arizona); 
Elke Rundensteiner (Worcester Polytechnic Institute)
<br />
          <div id="po50" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
In this talk, first author Dennis Hofmann will present a method for generating synthetic anomalies to improve anomaly detection. The approach tackles an emerging challenge: creating plausible anomalies without access to real examples. By leveraging the structure of inliers, the method generates realistic yet unseen anomalies, enabling improved detection in complex real-world scenarios. The talk is based on a paper that is currently under review.
               </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

               <tr>
                  <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">51</td>
        <td>
          <strong>GNN Training with Graph Summarization</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po51')">Toggle Abstract</a> Yuhang Song (Boston University)*; 
Naima Abrar (Boston University); 
Vasiliki Kalavri (Boston University)
<br />
          <div id="po51" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Training Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) on large-scale graphs is often limited by high computational cost and memory usage. Real-world graphs can contain millions or billions of edges, making end-to-end training prohibitively expensive. A common strategy to address this challenge is graph summarization, where the graph is reduced prior to training.<br/><br/>However, existing graph summarization methods are often dataset- or model-specific. A technique that performs well on one dataset may fail to reduce size effectively on another, while a method that preserves accuracy for one GNN architecture may significantly degrade performance for others. This variability highlights the lack of a unified way to evaluate summarization methods and makes it difficult for practitioners to choose an appropriate approach. <br/><br/>These challenges motivate the need for a standardized experimental framework that enables systematic comparison of graph summarization techniques across datasets and GNN models.<br/><br/>We propose a generic experimental framework that can incorporate both existing and future graph summarization techniques. The framework is designed to evaluate summarization methods consistently across multiple datasets and GNN architectures. Specifically, it enables direct comparison along three key dimensions: graph reduction ratio, computational efficiency, and accuracy preservation after training on the summarized graph.<br/>
              </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

               <!-- <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Scalable Image AI via Self-Designing Storage</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po52')">Toggle Abstract</a> Utku Sirin (Harvard University)*; 

<br />
          <div id="po52" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Image AI has the potential to improve every aspect of human life. Image AI, however, is very expensive. We identify that the root cause of the problem is a long-overlooked and largely unexplored dimension: storage. Most images today are stored as JPEG files. JPEG is designed for digital photography. It maximally compresses images with minimal loss in visual quality. We observe that JPEG is a fixed design. AI problems, however, are diverse; every problem is unique in terms of how data should be stored and processed. Using a fixed design, such as JPEG, for all problems results in excessive data movements and costly image AI systems. This paper presents Image Calculator, a self-designing storage system that finds the optimal storage for a given image AI task. The Image Calculator achieves this by identifying design primitives for image storage and creating a design space comprising thousands of storage formats based on these design primitives, each capable of storing and representing data differently, with varying accuracy, inference and training time, as well as space consumption trade-offs. It efficiently searches within this design space by building performance models and using locality among its storage formats. It exploits the inherent frequency structure in image data to efficiently serve inference and training requests. We evaluate the Image Calculator across a diverse set of datasets, tasks, models, and hardware. We show that Image Calculator can generate storage formats that reduce end-to-end inference and training times by up to 14.2x and consumed space by up to 8.2x with little or no loss in accuracy, compared to state-of-the-art image storage formats.
                </div>
        </td>
      </tr> -->

               <tr>
                  <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">53</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Making Query Accelerators Practical</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po53')">Toggle Abstract</a> Geoffrey Yu (Massachusetts Institute of Technology)*; 
Ryan Marcus (University of Pennsylvania); 
Tim Kraska (Massachusetts Institute of Technology)
<br />
          <div id="po53" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Relational database management systems (RDBMSes) provide a general-purpose way to query data, but have a lower performance compared to custom-built solutions for individual queries. As a toy example, consider a group by over a few known a priori groups (e.g., grouping by gender). While an RDBMS would likely use a hash map to do the grouping, a faster method would hardcode the expected groups into the query executor to avoid a hash map altogether. Yet, such workload-specific techniques, which we call query accelerators, are not widely used in practice. They trade away too much generality when used alone and are hard to add to an RDBMS because the engineering effort, optimizer changes, and lack of portability often make integration a non-starter.<br/><br/>We propose Tailwind: a new practical and non-invasive external query planner that brings accelerators into any RDBMS that supports data import/export. Users declare their accelerators’ semantics (i.e., what they compute) using abstract logical plans (ALPs): a new mostly-declarative abstraction over relational operators built on regular tree expressions. Using a new featurization, Tailwind then expresses the ALPs as neural networks to learn the accelerators’ performance offline. At runtime, Tailwind sits atop an RDBMS. It transparently rewrites queries to run across one or more accelerators when predicted to be beneficial, and falls back to the underlying RDBMS when not. On Redshift and DuckDB with a library of four diverse accelerators, Tailwind accelerates TPC-H queries by 1.35× on average (up to 71×).
              </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

               <tr>
                  <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">54</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Incremental Package Maintenance under Small Data Perturbations.</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po54')">Toggle Abstract</a> VASILEIOS VITTIS (University of Massachussetts Amherst)*; 

<br />
          <div id="po54" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Package queries let decision makers select an optimal set of tuples that satisfies linear constraints while optimizing a linear objective, capturing tasks such as portfolio construction, supply selection, and scheduling. The practical bottleneck is dynamism: real data changes continuously, and recomputing an optimal package from scratch after every update is too slow for real use.<br/>We introduce Incremental Package Maintenance (IPM) for small data perturbations. IPM maintains a compact K-skyband candidate set via dominance-based pruning and updates it efficiently under insertions, deletions, and updates; the ILP is solved only over this set. When K is derived from the package count bounds and repetition cap, the skyband is guaranteed to contain an optimal package, preserving correctness while avoiding full recomputation.<br/>Experiments on large datasets with continuous update streams show order-of-magnitude faster maintenance than recomputation, often with sub-second update times and substantial reductions in ILP size, making package queries practical for real-time decision support.
                 </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

               <tr>
                  <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">55</td>
        <td>
          <strong>ReCAP: A New Abstraction for Efficient Path-Query Processing in Relational Database Systems</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po55')">Toggle Abstract</a> Diego Rivera Correa (Northeastern University)*; 
Mirek Riedewald (Northeastern University); 

<br />
          <div id="po55" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Conventional wisdom dictates that specialized data management engines can outperform general-purpose database management systems (DBMS) by leveraging application-specific techniques and optimizations. To this end, both academia and industry have invested heavily in graph-query processing, resulting in the recent GQL language standard and state-of-the-art graph DBMS such as Neo4j, Memgraph, Kuzu, and MilleniumDB. Much of this effort has focused on path queries on property graphs, which are cumbersome to express in SQL (hence specialized graph languages) and benefit from custom data management techniques (hence specialized graph engines). We present strong evidence based on a prototype implementation in DuckDB that relational DBMS achieve competitive performance for regular path queries (RPQ) and even outperform state-of-the-art graph DBMS by a factor up to 17,800 times for path queries with constraints on edge and/or vertex properties. Hence we argue that it may be time for database researchers and practitioners to redirect efforts from specialized graph DBMS toward (1) automatically mapping queries expressed in graph-specific languages into SQL and (2) effective optimization of the resulting SQL queries in relational DBMS. As a first step toward these goals, we present ReCAP. It combines classic non-deterministic state machines with a dictionary-based memory, providing an aggregate-like abstraction to specify path queries where constraints on both labels and properties can be pushed deep into the relational query plan.
                </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

               <tr>
                  <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">56</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Querying OS Kernel Telemetry With BQL</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po56')">Toggle Abstract</a> Franco Solleza (Brown University)*; 
Faizah Naqvi (Brown University); 
Morgan Borjigin-Wang (Brown University); 
Justus Adam (Brown University); 
Malte Schwarzkopf (Brown University); 
Akshay Narayan (Brown University); 
Nesime Tatbul (Intel/MIT); 
Andrew Crotty (Northwestern University)
<br />
          <div id="po56" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
eBPF is an OS kernel technology that promises unprecedented access to application and in-kernel telemetry. Unfortunately, developing custom eBPF programs is difficult today. Complex and unstable APIs, a restrictive verifier, and hard-to-predict performance effects mean that it can take hours of programming effort to answer a simple query! Instead, software developers and observability engineers resort to off-the-shelf programs which severely limit eBPF’s promise, or specialized domain specific languages that still require eBPF expertise to use. <br/><br/>In this poster, we present BQL, a new telemetry system that accomplishes eBPF’s promise of unprecedented access to telemetry without needing eBPF expertise. A BQL user (e.g., an observability engineer) specifies the in-kernel telemetry as an SQL-like query. BQL then takes this query, automatically generates eBPF programs, and installs the program into the kernel. A BQL user can also create views that capture the semantics of in-kernel telemetry. With BQL, a user should only worry about what data they want instead of how to get it.
                </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

               <tr>
                  <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">57</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Adversarial Benchmark Generation</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po57')">Toggle Abstract</a> Jeffrey Tao (University of Pennsylvania)*; 
Yimeng Zeng (University of Pennsylvania); 
Haydn Jones (University of Pennsylvania); 
Natalie Maus (University of Pennsylvania); 
Jacob Gardner (University of Pennsylvania); 
Ryan Marcus (University of Pennsylvania)
<br />
          <div id="po57" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Database benchmarks have increasingly emphasized realism, generating queries that mirror production workloads in order to develop and tune query optimizers. However, even simple queries can expose fundamental weaknesses in optimizers. We present an adversarial approach to benchmark generation that systematically searches for queries where database systems fail to optimize effectively.<br/><br/>We achieve this by building upon recent work on offline optimization, which uses Bayesian optimization to find highly optimized plans for a given query. We extend this to search the joint space of queries and plans to find query-plan pairs where the performance gap between the DBMS's default plan and the Bayesian-optimized plan is high. This search process produces a set of queries, with accompanying witness plans that have better performance, where the DBMS demonstrably underperforms. We constrain this search process to simple SPJA queries, focusing on finding performance bugs in join orderings.<br/><br/>We apply this technique to generate benchmarks on the IMDB and StackOverflow datasets against DuckDB and produce dozens of queries where the default plan underperforms by 2x in relative terms and tens of seconds in absolute terms. Our technique is schema- and system-agnostic, providing a flexible and reusable mechanism for adversarially testing query optimizers.
                </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

               <tr>
                  <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">58</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Zero-downtime Reconfiguration for Long-running Distributed Stateful Dataflows</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po58')">Toggle Abstract</a> Lei Huang (Boston University)*; 
Sakshi Sharma (Boston University); 
Chenxu Yu (Boston University); 
Zikun Wang (Boston University); 
Yuanli Wang (Boston University); 
Zichen Zhu (Boston University); 
John Liagouris (Boston University); 
Vasiliki Kalavri (Boston University)
<br />
          <div id="po58" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Streaming analytics applications are long-running and dynamic, requiring frequent and adaptive reconfigurations to cope with workload fluctuations. However, reconfiguring stateful stream processing workloads often incurs significant downtime due to (i) draining in-flight data and (ii) migrating embedded states. Existing approaches attempt to mitigate this overhead by minimizing blocking operations and partitioning state migration into prioritized, fine-grained batches. Nevertheless, they still suffer from two major limitations: (i) at least one blocking phase in the rescaling protocol, inevitably causing latency spikes, and (ii) the need to migrate all states, regardless of their actual relevance. In this work, we aim to achieve zero-downtime reconfiguration through strictly non-blocking mechanisms. Specifically, we propose (i) peer data channels for fast load rerouting, and (ii) lazy state migration with fine-grained key ownership transfer, thereby eliminating performance degradation during reconfiguration. Our approach is particularly well-suited for bursty workloads and enhances resource efficiency by enabling flexible and transparent operations.
        </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

               <tr>
                  <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">59</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Multimodal Databases for Agentic AI Solutions in Healthcare</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po59')">Toggle Abstract</a> Jeff Fried (InterSystems)*
<br />
          <div id="po59" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Integrating multimodal databases with native vector datatypes in healthcare represents a major advance for managing and analyzing diverse clinical data (text, imaging, signals, and structured records) within a single, interoperable platform. In this talk, we introduce a fully integrated database designed to store, retrieve, and process complex multimodal datasets efficiently, enabling fast semantic search, similarity joins, and cross-referencing across systems. Building on this foundation, we show how vector-enabled data platforms can power agentic AI: goal-driven systems that plan, use tools, and execute multi-step workflows (e.g., chart review, cohort discovery, prior-authorization preparation, clinical trial matching) with controlled access to data and services.<br/><br/>A key challenge with agentic systems is traceability and operational control—understanding why an agent took an action, what data it accessed, which tools it invoked, and how intermediate outputs influenced decisions. We present a tracing facility that captures and persists all agent events (messages, tool calls, retrieved artifacts, and decisions) into the database to support development, debugging, auditing, and online tuning. This is especially valuable in healthcare, where workflows span many heterogeneous systems and require strong governance, reproducibility, and accountability.<br/><br/>We also provide an option to run models locally rather than relying on public LLM endpoints. Local execution strengthens privacy by keeping sensitive data on-site, reducing exposure to third parties, and supporting compliance with regulations such as HIPAA—while enabling consistent, low-latency feedback loops for clinical environments. We conclude with a live demo of the system and highlight representative clinical and research applications.<br/>
               </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

               <tr>
                  <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">60</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Optimizing ACORN performance using an integrated vector database</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po60')">Toggle Abstract</a> Jeff Fried (InterSystems)*
<br />
          <div id="po60" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Modern applications increasingly rely on mixed-modality data and require hybrid retrieval: jointly searching high-dimensional vectors (embeddings of images, text, and video) alongside traditional structured predicates (attributes, filters, and keywords). Recent work on constrained approximate nearest-neighbor search—most notably the ACORN (ANN Constraint-Optimized Retrieval Network) family of techniques—has renewed interest in database-native approaches that can execute these mixed queries efficiently and at scale.<br/><br/>This paper presents an advanced implementation of ACORN-1 within an integrated vector database built on a “Post-Relational” architecture [1]. Unlike bolt-on extensions or standalone ANN libraries, our design is inherently multi-model and optimizer-aware: ACORN is integrated into the query planner to preserve high performance under selective filters and complex mixed predicates. We expose hybrid retrieval through an intuitive SQL interface, while tightly coupling ingestion, embedding creation, indexing, and retrieval inside the data engine—abstracting pipeline complexity without sacrificing performance.<br/>
   </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

               <tr>
                  <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">62</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Data as the foundation for Intelligent Agents</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po62')">Toggle Abstract</a> Sheeba Prakash (IBM)*
<br />
          <div id="po62" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Data serves as the foundational pillar of Agentic AI systems, which are designed to operate autonomously by setting goals, evaluating options, making decisions, and executing actions with minimal human intervention. At the heart of these systems lies data, enabling agents to perceive their environment, reason effectively, and learn from outcomes. Without high-quality, well-governed data, Agentic AI cannot function safely or deliver reliable results.<br/>Agentic AI systems depend on multiple data types to achieve autonomy and adaptability. Training data establishes initial capabilities by teaching patterns, behaviors, and constraints. Real-time data allows agents to respond dynamically to changing conditions, while feedback data supports continuous learning from successes and failures. Contextual data defines operational rules, goals, and limitations, and historical data aids in planning and predictive modeling. Together, these data streams form the knowledge base that drives perception, reasoning, and decision-making.<br/>The quality and reliability of data are critical. Incomplete, outdated, or biased data can lead to unsafe or incorrect autonomous actions, such as a self-driving car taking an unintended route or an M&amp;A forecasting system producing inaccurate projections due to misaligned processes. Poor data management also reinforces bias—for example, job recommendation systems perpetuating gender bias by repeatedly favoring male candidates for technical roles. In M&amp;A contexts, bias can emerge when systems overfit to one company’s processes, ignoring the acquired entity’s practices. These failures erode trust in AI systems, especially when transparency, fairness, and reliability are lacking.<br/>Beyond operational risks, poor data governance raises significant ethical and legal concerns. Ethical issues include bias and discrimination, privacy infringement, lack of explainability, and unclear accountability. Legal implications span employment discrimination, data protection and security, intellectual property rights, product liability, and professional ethics. Robust data governance frameworks, combined with observability and validation mechanisms, are essential to detect anomalies, prevent drift, and ensure compliance with regulatory standards.<br/>Data is the fundamental building block for Agentic AI. Ensuring its quality, integrity, and ethical use is paramount for building trustworthy, transparent, and responsible autonomous solutions that can operate safely and effectively in complex environments. Will discuss on this from CDO M&amp;A perspective and experience.<br/>
               </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

               <tr>
                  <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">64</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Accurate ML Processing under Real-Time Constraints</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po64')">Toggle Abstract</a> Phillip Hilliard (University of Pennsylvania)*; 
Rajeev Alur (University of Pennsylvania); 
Zachary Ives (University of Pennsylvania)
<br />
          <div id="po64" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Machine learning models provide opportunities for reactive insights, but they face constraints that limit their advantages when deploying for real-time applications. ML models are inaccurate, and this inaccuracy is not uniform; a population of patients being monitored for medical insights may have members join or leave, or those members may experience different classes of symptoms for which ML models have different degrees of accuracy. ML models are not without cost; an API for LLM invocations has a monetary cost for each prompt, and this depletes the limited budget available for any application, even as new inputs come in with their own varied costs. Real-time results do not retain their value indefinitely; visual recognition tasks need to be able to keep up with the volume of images and the changing number of subjects in those images, in order for the application to be able to handle all its inputs.<br/><br/>ML model deployments have limited resources, and real-time deployments that cannot handle their throughput requirements with the available resources must use some less costly fallback behavior, or risk falling behind. Examples of these include ignoring an input, using a default value, or using the last-available value, all of which lose some accuracy. The alternative is to use different versions of ML models that make other tradeoffs. Smaller ML models may be less accurate for some parts of the input distribution, but are able to handle a larger volume or velocity of inputs, allowing them to maintain an acceptable level of accuracy under stricter constraints. When greater resources are available, larger ML models typically have higher costs, but provide better accuracy.<br/><br/>We show that we can improve performance over the accuracy-throughput tradeoff curve by adapting to choose between a selection of models in real time. We do this by combining several components when we receive a batch of inputs. First, we use a light-weight pre-classifier to identify buckets, or broad categories of inputs over which the accuracy of various models may change. Second, we forecast the distribution of buckets that we expect for future items, based on the past bucket distribution. Third, we estimate the budget of time or money available to execute the current and future items models. Fourth, we conduct a scheduling task to approximate the best distribution that maps items to available ML models, based on the items’ buckets. Fifth, we apply that distribution to our inputs and adjust our estimates based on the observed costs.<br/><br/>By comparing the individual models against a strategy that uses our framework with a greedy scheduling algorithm, we show robustness to distribution shift and adaptivity to changing input velocity. We show this across different modalities with different budget-throughput demands, and we also show small advantages in some places, despite the simple nature of our lightweight pre-classifier.
      </div>
        </td>
      </tr>


               <tr>
                  <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">65</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Towards Better Vector Set Indexes for Semantic Retrieval</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po65')">Toggle Abstract</a> Ziyu Zhang (MIT)*; 
Vihan Lakshman (MIT); 
Michael Cafarella (MIT); 
Julian Shun (MIT)
<br />
          <div id="po65" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Recent advances in semantic retrieval increasingly rely on multi-vector representations, motivated by late-interaction models for challenging tasks such as long-document retrieval and multi-hop and reasoning-intensive question answering. While efficient vector set indexing techniques have been studied, existing works largely focus on workloads induced by late-interaction embeddings, leaving broader semantic retrieval workloads under-explored.<br/>This work investigates how vector set search can improve semantic retrieval beyond late-interaction models. We explore two directions. First, we propose a query-workload-aware document expansion method that discovers query intents and represents each document as a set of intent-conditioned embeddings, helping bridge the semantic–reasoning gap between queries and documents. Second, we show how vector sets can model fine-grained, combinatorial attribute matchings by representing entities and attributes as vector set members and tuning MaxSim-style similarity operators.<br/>We evaluate both the retrieval effectiveness of these representations and the implications they pose for vector set indexing design. This poster will present preliminary results from an ongoing work.
      </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

               <tr>
                  <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">66</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Semantic Indexing for Unstructured Data Management at Scale</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po66')">Toggle Abstract</a> Varun Jana (University of Pennsylvania)*; 
Zack Ives (University of Pennsylvania)
<br />
          <div id="po66" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Recently introduced LLM powered data systems have demonstrated several promising avenues for processing collections of unstructured data at scale. However, calling an LLM to process tuples (in fact, a full cartesian product for a semantic join) based on a natural language predicate is prohibitively expensive to scale. Several systems have proposed scaling techniques like cascading models, semantic similarity, or other extraction based methods, but these methods do not generalize well to full workloads.<br/><br/>We introduce System S, the first semantic data management system that, akin to classical DBMSes, manages a semantic data catalog with automatically generated semantic indexes, and uses them to synthesize highly efficient physical execution plans end-to-end. Over several workloads, we demonstrate that our semantic indexer can automatically select the right features to index for a given query (or workload), and effectively select and reuse them on new queries. Our main insights are that context needs to be taken into account in codesigning an index and the queries that use it; and the space of semantic indexes grows sublinearly with the query workload, following a classical zipfian distribution of concepts such as those in natural language. Within this context, we focus on the semantic indexing selection and the physical planner.<br/><br/>System S features a full operator planner capable of producing and selecting between synergistic query plans to optimize for various cost and quality metrics, taking advantage of the semantic indices. These physical plans, rewritten to use semantic indices for pruning, also feature cheaper (non-LLM) proxies, combining knowledge from dedicated ETL pipeline development and are synthesized on-demand as kernels. Our automatically generated substitute physical plans can scale cheaply while improving latency by several orders of magnitude. <br/><br/>Previous work has shown the effectiveness of combining the outputs LLM generated annotators with weak learning can outperform LLM based extractors. Based on the observation that each semantic operator can be decomposed into only a few physical kernels that are highly synergistic, we introduce a weak learning based physical output aggregator.<br/><br/>We rigorously test System S on a diverse set of 12 workloads*, optimizing for various metrics such as cost and quality, demonstrating state-of-the-art performance while being able to reduce costs by 99% and query latency by more than 200x. We show that by using contextual information from the semantic catalog, such as existing semantic indices, context-aware planning and combining the outputs of each kernel (or annotator) with weak learning methods, System S can achieve comparable performance to fully LLM based methods for a variety of queries.
          </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

               <tr>
                  <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">67</td>
        <td>
          <strong>SPEAR: Making Prompts First-Class Citizens for Adaptive LLM Pipelines</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po67')">Toggle Abstract</a> Uğur Çetintemel (Brown University); 
Shu Chen (Brown University); 
Alexander Lee (Brown University); 
Deepti Raghavan (Brown University); 
Duo Lu (Brown University)*; 
Andrew Crotty (Northwestern University)
<br />
          <div id="po67" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
In this poster, we will present SPEAR (Structured Prompt Execution and Adaptive Refinement), a new approach to prompt management that elevates prompts from brittle, opaque strings to first-class citizens within LLM pipelines. We describe how SPEAR treats prompts as structured data objects organized into versioned "views," enabling modular reuse, introspection, and provenance tracking similar to database views. We will showcase SPEAR's execution model, which supports adaptive prompt refinement, allowing prompts to evolve dynamically during execution based on runtime feedback, and policy-driven control, a mechanism for specifying automatic refinement logic via declarative rules. Finally, we will demonstrate how this structured approach enables novel end-to-end optimization opportunities, such as automated refinement based on input/output conditions, which our preliminary results show can significantly improve both accuracy and cost-efficiency compared to existing frameworks like DSPy.<br/><br/>This talk is based on our recent paper, which will appear in CIDR 2026.
               </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

            <tr>
                <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">68</td>
        <td>
          <strong>SuDocu+: Refining Subjective Summarization Intent Through Natural Language Conversation</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po68')">Toggle Abstract</a> Aryan Deshpande (University of Massachusets Amherst)*; 
Vasileios Vittis (University of Massachusetts Amherst); 
Alexandra Meliou (University of Massachusetts Amherst); 
Peter Haas (University of Massachusetts Amherst)
<br />
          <div id="po68" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Subjective extractive summarization is critical for systems that help users digest large corpora—news, reports, and research, where what “matters” is refined over time. SuDocu is an existing subjective summarization system that works in the following manner: first, the user creates example summaries that reflect their intent. Then, from those learned examples, SuDocu captures the intent as a package query. Finally, the package query is solved to produce the final summary. This approach is feasible for just one summary generation, but in reality, users refine their intent iteratively until they are satisfied. Requiring a new set of examples for every refinement is slow, cognitively heavy, and assumes the user already understands the latent topic structure of the corpus.<br/><br/>We present SuDocu+, which adds a conversational refinement channel to SuDocu. In the original SuDocu, a user must repeat the cumbersome process of generating completely new examples every time they want to refine the summary. In SuDocu+, the process is much more intuitive: users issue lightweight natural - language commands (e.g., “more nature, less politics”), and an intent-to-constraint mapping module converts them into principled updates to the underlying package- query constraints. The mapping can be implemented with LLM- based few-shot prompting that produces structured constraint edits, preserving the prescriptive guarantees of package-query optimization while enabling low-effort iterative refinement.<br/><br/>We demonstrate SuDocu+ in a topic-balanced summarization case study and compare two strategies: a multi-step pipeline that builds separate topic-aligned exemplars and a single-shot hybrid pipeline that starts from mixed-topic exemplars. The single-shot approach is more practical, yielding better balanced coverage under rank-based diagnostics with less computation and interaction. These results highlight the limits of exemplar-only workflows and motivate SuDocu+ as a scalable, user-friendly alternative for intent-driven summarization.<br/><br/>References<br/>[1] Anna Fariha, Matteo Brucato, Peter J. Haas, and Alexandra Meliou. SuDocu: Summarizing documents by example. Proc. VLDB Endow., 13(12):2861–2864, 2020. doi: 10.14778/3415478.3415494. URL http://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol13/p2861-fariha.pdf.
               </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

            <!-- <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Database Research needs an Abstract Relational Query Language</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po69')">Toggle Abstract</a> Wolfgang Gatterbauer (Northeastern University)*
<br />
          <div id="po69" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
For decades, SQL has been the default language for composing queries, but it is increasingly used as an artifact to be read and verified rather than authored. With Large Language Models (LLMs), queries are increasingly machine-generated, while humans read, validate, and debug them. This shift turns relational query languages into interfaces for back-and-forth communication about intent, which will lead to a rethinking of relational language and interface design. <br/><br/>We argue that this rethinking needs support from an Abstract Relational Query Language (ARQL): a semantics-first reference metalanguage that separates query intent from user-facing syntax and makes underlying relational patterns explicit and comparable across user-facing languages. An ARQL separates a query into (𝑖) a relational core (the compositional structure that determines intent), (𝑖𝑖) modalities (alternative representations of that core tailored to different audiences), and (𝑖𝑖𝑖) conventions (orthogonal environment-level semantic parameters under which the core is interpreted, e.g., set vs. bag semantics, or treatment of null values). Usability for humans or machines then depends less on choosing a particular language and more on choosing an appropriate modality. Comparing languages becomes a question of which relational patterns they support and what conventions they choose.<br/><br/>We present Abstract Relational Calculus (ARC), a strict generalization of Tuple Relational Calculus (TRC), as an instance of ARQL. ARC comes in three modalities: (𝑖) a comprehension-style textual notation, (𝑖𝑖) an Abstract Language Tree (ALT) for machine reasoning about meaning, and (𝑖𝑖𝑖) a diagrammatic hierarchical-graph (higraph) representation for humans. ARC provides the missing vocabulary and acts as a Rosetta Stone for relational querying. Because its ALT makes binding, scoping, and grouping structure explicit in a small, reusable operator vocabulary, it is a natural intermediate target for automated query generation and validation, including NL2SQL systems that translate natural language into a semantic query representation and then render it to SQL.
              </div>
        </td>
      </tr> -->

            <tr>
                <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">70</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Deep Research is the New Analytics System: Towards Building the Runtime for AI-Driven Analytics</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po70')">Toggle Abstract</a> Matthew Russo (MIT)*; 
Tim Kraska (MIT)
<br />
          <div id="po70" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
With advances in large language models (LLMs), researchers are creating new systems that can perform AI-driven analytics over large unstructured datasets. Recent work has explored executing such analytics queries using semantic operators---a declarative set of AI-powered data transformations with natural language specifications. However, even when optimized, these operators can be expensive to execute on millions of records and their iterator execution semantics make them ill-suited for interactive data analytics tasks. In another line of work, Deep Research systems have demonstrated an ability to answer natural language question(s) over large datasets. These systems use one or more LLM agent(s) to plan their execution, process the dataset(s), and iteratively refine their answer. However, these systems do not explicitly optimize their query plans which can lead to poor plan execution. In order for AI-driven analytics to excel, we need a runtime which combines the optimized execution of semantic operators with the flexibility and more dynamic execution of Deep Research systems. As a first step towards this vision, we build a prototype which enables Deep Research agents to write and execute optimized semantic operator programs. We evaluate our prototype and demonstrate that it can outperform a handcrafted semantic operator program and open Deep Research systems on two basic queries. Compared to a standard open Deep Research agent, our prototype achieves up to 1.95x better F1-score. Furthermore, even if we give the agent access to semantic operators as tools, our prototype still achieves cost and runtime savings of up to 76.8% and 72.7% thanks to its optimized execution.
               </div>
        </td>
      </tr>

                  <tr>
                <td style="text-align: center; vertical-align: top;">71</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Cost-Aware Hybrid Search with Automated Design of Metadata for NL Queries</strong><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po71')">Toggle Abstract</a> Zhuohan Gu (MIT CSAIL)*
Matthew Russo (MIT CSAIL); 
Joyce Qu (MIT CSAIL); 
Omar Khattab (MIT CSAIL); 
Tim Kraska (MIT CSAIL); 
Tianyu Li (MIT CSAIL); 
Samuel Madden (MIT CSAIL)
<br />
          <div id="po71" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Automatically designing the metadata and query planning that underpin hybrid search for natural-language queries is a non-trivial task. A judicious design must be cost-driven, informed by the workload, and able to exploit both structural and semantic regularities in the corpus. Today’s vector databases and LLM-based retrieval stacks expose dense or sparse similarity search with optional metadata filters, but leave practitioners to handcraft schemas, populate attributes, and manually stitch together keyword, vector, and filter operators. As a result, complex information needs that mix fuzzy semantics with precise constraints and set operations are often served by ad hoc pipelines that are difficult to tune, brittle under workload drift, and expensive to operate. In this work, we present Nous, the first end-to-end system that automates the heavy lifting of hybrid search: it automatically extracts and materializes structural and semantic metadata, builds and maintains the corresponding index portfolio, caches and reuses hybrid query plans to keep LLMs out of the critical path, and adapts its design based on observed cost signals.
               </div>
        </td>
      </tr>